1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:01,716
You wanted to be a farmer,

2
00:00:01,716 --> 00:00:02,795
like your father.

3
00:00:02,795 --> 00:00:04,572
A small field, a few cows,

4
00:00:04,572 --> 00:00:07,691
and the church bell across the bay on a Sunday morning.

5
00:00:08,146 --> 00:00:10,487
Instead, it is barely light,

6
00:00:10,487 --> 00:00:14,091
your wrists ache from the rope, and you are sitting

7
00:00:14,091 --> 00:00:16,495
at the foot of a mast on a Norse ship,

8
00:00:16,495 --> 00:00:19,421
watching your coast shrink to a grey line.

9
00:00:19,795 --> 00:00:22,588
The Viking legend always has a price.

10
00:00:22,588 --> 00:00:24,719
Somebody always pays it.

11
00:00:24,719 --> 00:00:28,108
This summer, that somebody is you.

12
00:00:28,108 --> 00:00:29,915
Imagine you are Colm,

13
00:00:29,915 --> 00:00:33,606
one of the two villagers taken in Bjorn's raid,

14
00:00:33,606 --> 00:00:34,890
two chapters ago.

15
00:00:34,890 --> 00:00:36,920
Like Eirik and Aslaug,

16
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:38,431
you are an imagined person,

17
00:00:38,431 --> 00:00:42,563
built from what archaeology, laws and written sources tell us,

18
00:00:42,563 --> 00:00:44,923
not a real man from history.

19
00:00:44,923 --> 00:00:46,154
You are about twenty,

20
00:00:46,154 --> 00:00:48,695
with short dark hair, no beard,

21
00:00:48,695 --> 00:00:51,906
a rough undyed tunic, and the shoes you happened to be

22
00:00:51,906 --> 00:00:53,870
wearing when the bell started ringing.

23
00:00:53,870 --> 00:00:56,516
Everything else you owned is behind you.

24
00:00:56,516 --> 00:00:59,284
You recognise a few faces at the oars.

25
00:00:59,284 --> 00:01:01,067
One of them is a red-bearded man

26
00:01:01,067 --> 00:01:03,360
with a fresh cut across his forearm.

27
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,032
He does not look at you.

28
00:01:05,032 --> 00:01:06,736
Most of them do not.

29
00:01:07,141 --> 00:01:08,416
Popular culture rarely

30
00:01:08,416 --> 00:01:09,991
shows people like you.

31
00:01:10,589 --> 00:01:12,428
In films, you are the figure

32
00:01:12,428 --> 00:01:15,319
carrying firewood in the background.

33
00:01:15,319 --> 00:01:16,185
In the sagas,

34
00:01:16,185 --> 00:01:18,650
you are often a joke or a villain.

35
00:01:19,088 --> 00:01:21,107
But the Norse had a word for you,

36
00:01:21,107 --> 00:01:23,367
and English borrowed it: thrall.

37
00:01:23,902 --> 00:01:25,633
Today, when a story holds an

38
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:26,826
audience in thrall,

39
00:01:26,826 --> 00:01:30,025
the phrase still carries the memory of your new job.

40
00:01:30,025 --> 00:01:31,894
You may have heard that Viking

41
00:01:31,894 --> 00:01:33,883
thralls could earn their freedom.

42
00:01:33,883 --> 00:01:35,062
Some did.

43
00:01:35,062 --> 00:01:37,018
This chapter is about the fine print.

44
00:01:37,018 --> 00:01:39,140
And at the end of this story,

45
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:41,366
we will visit a group of islands off Iceland

46
00:01:41,366 --> 00:01:44,018
that still carry the name of runaway thralls,

47
00:01:44,018 --> 00:01:46,358
and find out what happened to them.

48
00:01:46,358 --> 00:01:49,032
The ship does not take you to Norway.

49
00:01:49,032 --> 00:01:49,825
Not yet.

50
00:01:50,872 --> 00:01:53,999
On the way home, Bjorn stops at a trading place,

51
00:01:53,999 --> 00:01:54,937
and you find out

52
00:01:54,937 --> 00:01:56,427
what you are worth to him:

53
00:01:56,427 --> 00:01:59,430
a few pieces of silver, weighed on a scale.

54
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:01,227
You are not a rare case.

55
00:02:01,761 --> 00:02:03,890
The National Museum of Denmark notes

56
00:02:03,890 --> 00:02:06,199
that Vikings acquired slaves mainly on

57
00:02:06,199 --> 00:02:09,534
expeditions to Eastern Europe and the British Isles,

58
00:02:09,534 --> 00:02:12,019
and sold them at trading centres

59
00:02:12,019 --> 00:02:15,217
such as Hedeby, in what is now northern Germany,

60
00:02:15,217 --> 00:02:18,318
and Bolghar, on the river Volga.

61
00:02:18,318 --> 00:02:20,308
Some captures were enormous.

62
00:02:20,308 --> 00:02:22,316
The Annals of Ulster,

63
00:02:22,316 --> 00:02:25,126
a year-by-year chronicle kept in Ireland,

64
00:02:25,126 --> 00:02:27,253
record that in eight hundred and seventy-one,

65
00:02:27,253 --> 00:02:32,410
two Norse kings returned to Dublin from Scotland with two hundred ships,

66
00:02:32,410 --> 00:02:34,973
bringing a great number of Angles,

67
00:02:34,973 --> 00:02:37,998
Britons and Picts into captivity.

68
00:02:38,597 --> 00:02:40,941
Dublin, then ruled by Norse kings,

69
00:02:40,941 --> 00:02:44,597
grew into a major slave market of the Irish Sea.

70
00:02:44,597 --> 00:02:47,231
The historian Poul Holm argues that its

71
00:02:47,231 --> 00:02:50,130
trade grew busiest in the second half

72
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:52,616
of the ten hundreds, long after your time.

73
00:02:52,616 --> 00:02:54,821
The business you have just

74
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:56,996
entered will outlive you by generations.

75
00:02:56,996 --> 00:02:58,931
It was old in the east as well.

76
00:02:58,931 --> 00:03:01,740
A biography of the missionary Ansgar,

77
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:04,420
written soon after his death in

78
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:05,208
eight hundred and sixty-five,

79
00:03:05,208 --> 00:03:08,060
says that at the Swedish trading town of Birka,

80
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:10,186
many Christians were held captive.

81
00:03:10,186 --> 00:03:12,492
The Swedish History Museum describes

82
00:03:12,492 --> 00:03:15,781
both Birka and Hedeby as slave markets.

83
00:03:15,781 --> 00:03:18,491
The man who buys you deals in everything:

84
00:03:18,491 --> 00:03:21,905
furs, salt, iron, and people.

85
00:03:22,359 --> 00:03:23,783
He takes you north along the

86
00:03:23,783 --> 00:03:25,900
Norwegian coast and sells you again.

87
00:03:26,354 --> 00:03:27,619
You do not understand a

88
00:03:27,619 --> 00:03:28,883
word of the bargaining.

89
00:03:28,883 --> 00:03:30,553
You understand the scale.

90
00:03:31,088 --> 00:03:32,652
Your new owner is Hallvard,

91
00:03:32,652 --> 00:03:35,746
a wealthy farmer from a valley in western Norway.

92
00:03:35,746 --> 00:03:38,500
He is a big man with a black beard,

93
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:40,924
a dark blue cloak with a fur collar,

94
00:03:40,924 --> 00:03:42,591
and a silver brooch that he touches

95
00:03:42,591 --> 00:03:46,246
whenever he thinks about money, which is often.

96
00:03:46,246 --> 00:03:49,346
Hallvard looks at your hands and your shoulders,

97
00:03:49,346 --> 00:03:51,282
the way a farmer looks at a horse.

98
00:03:51,817 --> 00:03:52,615
Then he pays.

99
00:03:52,615 --> 00:03:54,154
The whole thing takes less

100
00:03:54,154 --> 00:03:56,233
time than choosing a horse would.

101
00:03:56,233 --> 00:03:57,732
The valley he brings you

102
00:03:57,732 --> 00:03:59,921
to is quiet, green and wet.

103
00:03:59,921 --> 00:04:02,264
You have never seen it before.

104
00:04:02,799 --> 00:04:04,493
You will soon learn that one of its

105
00:04:04,493 --> 00:04:06,687
smaller farms belongs to a man with

106
00:04:06,687 --> 00:04:08,976
a red beard and a cut on his forearm.

107
00:04:08,976 --> 00:04:10,523
On Hallvard's farm,

108
00:04:10,523 --> 00:04:12,064
you learn your place in the law,

109
00:04:12,064 --> 00:04:13,906
and it is barely a place at all.

110
00:04:14,537 --> 00:04:15,863
The National Museum of

111
00:04:15,863 --> 00:04:17,881
Denmark describes slaves as people

112
00:04:17,881 --> 00:04:19,963
without any personal rights.

113
00:04:19,963 --> 00:04:22,279
The Norwegian encyclopedia

114
00:04:22,279 --> 00:04:24,308
Store norske leksikon is even colder.

115
00:04:24,308 --> 00:04:26,616
In the oldest Nordic provincial laws,

116
00:04:26,616 --> 00:04:30,362
it says, a thrall was treated as movable property,

117
00:04:30,362 --> 00:04:31,915
with no legal standing.

118
00:04:31,915 --> 00:04:34,534
In the eyes of the law, you belong on the

119
00:04:34,534 --> 00:04:37,195
same list as Hallvard's carts and cattle.

120
00:04:37,616 --> 00:04:38,856
Your owner can punish you,

121
00:04:38,856 --> 00:04:41,943
and the museum notes that punishments could be severe.

122
00:04:41,943 --> 00:04:44,556
You cannot take a case to the assembly.

123
00:04:44,556 --> 00:04:47,392
Nobody there is waiting to hear your side.

124
00:04:47,392 --> 00:04:49,898
And your status does not end with you.

125
00:04:49,898 --> 00:04:51,782
If you ever have children here,

126
00:04:51,782 --> 00:04:54,986
historians note, they could inherit your thralldom.

127
00:04:54,986 --> 00:04:56,272
Not every thrall on this

128
00:04:56,272 --> 00:04:58,096
farm was captured abroad.

129
00:04:58,096 --> 00:04:59,611
You share a corner of the work

130
00:04:59,611 --> 00:05:01,723
shed with an older man called Ketil,

131
00:05:02,098 --> 00:05:04,241
a Norwegian with grey stubble,

132
00:05:04,241 --> 00:05:07,126
a limp, and a tunic patched so many

133
00:05:07,126 --> 00:05:09,200
times that the patches have patches.

134
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:10,509
He was born free.

135
00:05:10,915 --> 00:05:12,512
Years ago, in a bad winter,

136
00:05:12,512 --> 00:05:13,872
he borrowed grain from

137
00:05:13,872 --> 00:05:16,115
Hallvard and could not pay it back.

138
00:05:16,115 --> 00:05:17,778
That really could happen.

139
00:05:17,778 --> 00:05:20,403
The Swedish History Museum notes that

140
00:05:20,403 --> 00:05:21,860
even a free person could enter

141
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:24,288
temporary thralldom through poverty or to

142
00:05:24,288 --> 00:05:27,097
settle a debt, and Store norske leksikon

143
00:05:27,097 --> 00:05:30,361
describes debt slaves in the oldest laws.

144
00:05:30,361 --> 00:05:32,467
The National Museum of Denmark adds

145
00:05:32,467 --> 00:05:34,857
that crimes such as murder and theft

146
00:05:34,857 --> 00:05:36,981
could also be punished with slavery.

147
00:05:36,981 --> 00:05:40,368
Ketil teaches you your first Norse words:

148
00:05:40,368 --> 00:05:43,339
work, fence, pig, hurry.

149
00:05:43,339 --> 00:05:45,289
He also teaches you the most

150
00:05:45,289 --> 00:05:47,528
useful skill on the farm:

151
00:05:47,528 --> 00:05:50,653
looking busy whenever Hallvard walks past.

152
00:05:51,123 --> 00:05:52,388
Norse poetry did not

153
00:05:52,388 --> 00:05:54,436
think much of either of you.

154
00:05:54,970 --> 00:05:56,180
The poem Rígsþula,

155
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:59,348
which imagines how the classes of society began,

156
00:05:59,348 --> 00:06:02,157
describes the very first thrall:

157
00:06:02,157 --> 00:06:04,441
dark hair, wrinkled skin,

158
00:06:04,441 --> 00:06:09,307
knotted knuckles, thick fingers, an ugly face and a bent back.

159
00:06:09,307 --> 00:06:11,254
Then it names his children.

160
00:06:11,254 --> 00:06:12,259
In translation,

161
00:06:12,259 --> 00:06:17,440
the names include Horsefly, Sluggard, Stumpy and Log.

162
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:20,027
The Swedish History Museum

163
00:06:20,027 --> 00:06:21,211
notes that the sagas,

164
00:06:21,211 --> 00:06:24,721
too, portray thralls as dark, ugly and foolish.

165
00:06:24,721 --> 00:06:27,641
Remember who wrote these texts.

166
00:06:27,641 --> 00:06:28,502
Not thralls.

167
00:06:28,502 --> 00:06:30,621
Historians warn that thralls in

168
00:06:30,621 --> 00:06:33,170
the sagas are often literary devices,

169
00:06:33,170 --> 00:06:34,627
shaped by the views of

170
00:06:34,627 --> 00:06:36,690
authors writing in the twelve hundreds.

171
00:06:36,690 --> 00:06:38,760
The people in the background of

172
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:41,047
the story never got to tell it.

173
00:06:41,047 --> 00:06:43,698
Your days begin before Hallvard's,

174
00:06:43,698 --> 00:06:45,084
and end after his.

175
00:06:45,084 --> 00:06:46,689
The work is whatever

176
00:06:46,689 --> 00:06:48,404
nobody free wants to do.

177
00:06:48,404 --> 00:06:51,682
The Swedish History Museum lists it plainly:

178
00:06:51,682 --> 00:06:52,952
repairing fences,

179
00:06:52,952 --> 00:06:57,233
fertilising fields, herding pigs and sheep, and cutting turf.

180
00:06:57,233 --> 00:06:59,767
The National Museum of Denmark says

181
00:06:59,767 --> 00:07:01,602
owners used slaves for the hardest

182
00:07:01,602 --> 00:07:04,323
and most unpleasant work in the fields.

183
00:07:04,323 --> 00:07:06,079
Rígsþula hands the thrall's

184
00:07:06,079 --> 00:07:08,531
children exactly the same chores.

185
00:07:08,531 --> 00:07:09,833
So you carry dung to the

186
00:07:09,833 --> 00:07:11,868
fields in a basket on your back.

187
00:07:11,868 --> 00:07:15,728
You cut turf, heavy wet blocks of earth and grass,

188
00:07:15,728 --> 00:07:17,632
and stack them to patch the roof.

189
00:07:18,054 --> 00:07:19,017
You mend fences,

190
00:07:19,017 --> 00:07:22,041
and Hallvard's pigs break them again by evening.

191
00:07:22,041 --> 00:07:22,577
The pigs,

192
00:07:22,577 --> 00:07:24,964
at least, seem to enjoy their work.

193
00:07:25,514 --> 00:07:27,356
Rígsþula's first thrall also

194
00:07:27,356 --> 00:07:29,728
hauls home firewood all day long.

195
00:07:29,728 --> 00:07:30,934
In that one detail,

196
00:07:30,934 --> 00:07:33,225
the poem gets you exactly right.

197
00:07:33,775 --> 00:07:35,177
Autumn brings the slaughter,

198
00:07:35,177 --> 00:07:37,121
and the farm prepares for winter.

199
00:07:37,559 --> 00:07:39,802
You carry meat to be dried and salted,

200
00:07:39,802 --> 00:07:41,483
and fish to be stacked.

201
00:07:41,809 --> 00:07:42,836
You do not decide

202
00:07:42,836 --> 00:07:44,375
what goes into the stores.

203
00:07:44,375 --> 00:07:45,668
You do find out who

204
00:07:45,668 --> 00:07:47,604
eats last when they run low.

205
00:07:47,604 --> 00:07:50,488
Winter is long, dark and smoky.

206
00:07:50,488 --> 00:07:53,721
Hallvard's household sits by the hearth,

207
00:07:53,721 --> 00:07:54,555
telling stories,

208
00:07:54,555 --> 00:07:56,947
and you start to follow a few words.

209
00:07:56,947 --> 00:07:58,416
Most of the stories are about

210
00:07:58,416 --> 00:08:00,256
men like Hallvard's grandfather,

211
00:08:00,256 --> 00:08:02,639
who sailed west and came home rich.

212
00:08:02,639 --> 00:08:04,595
Nobody explains where

213
00:08:04,595 --> 00:08:05,862
the silver came from.

214
00:08:05,862 --> 00:08:06,841
Nobody needs to.

215
00:08:06,841 --> 00:08:08,701
You are sitting in the corner,

216
00:08:08,701 --> 00:08:09,569
proof of it.

217
00:08:09,569 --> 00:08:10,903
Late in the winter,

218
00:08:10,903 --> 00:08:14,280
a sheep wanders down the valley and into Hallvard's yard.

219
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:15,581
It is a scruffy,

220
00:08:15,581 --> 00:08:19,399
determined animal with a gift for finding broken fences.

221
00:08:19,758 --> 00:08:21,628
Ketil recognises it at once.

222
00:08:21,628 --> 00:08:24,345
It belongs to the small farm up the hill.

223
00:08:24,345 --> 00:08:26,550
Hallvard sends you to take it back.

224
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:28,367
It is the first time you have

225
00:08:28,367 --> 00:08:29,871
walked anywhere alone since the raid.

226
00:08:29,871 --> 00:08:31,325
You think about running.

227
00:08:31,325 --> 00:08:32,946
Then you think about the snow,

228
00:08:32,946 --> 00:08:33,991
the fjord, the sea,

229
00:08:33,991 --> 00:08:36,408
and the fact that every farm in the valley

230
00:08:36,408 --> 00:08:38,326
knows exactly whose thrall you are.

231
00:08:38,326 --> 00:08:40,254
At the gate of the small farm,

232
00:08:40,254 --> 00:08:42,445
a man with a red beard is mending a fence.

233
00:08:42,787 --> 00:08:45,206
On his forearm is a long, pale scar.

234
00:08:45,206 --> 00:08:46,421
You know him.

235
00:08:46,794 --> 00:08:47,897
He was at the oars

236
00:08:47,897 --> 00:08:49,406
when you sat by the mast.

237
00:08:49,406 --> 00:08:51,095
He takes the sheep,

238
00:08:51,095 --> 00:08:54,136
thanks you briefly, and turns back to his fence.

239
00:08:54,136 --> 00:08:56,158
He does not know you at all.

240
00:08:56,158 --> 00:08:57,027
A woman in a mauve

241
00:08:57,027 --> 00:08:58,698
dress comes out of the house.

242
00:08:59,153 --> 00:09:00,461
She is expecting a child,

243
00:09:00,461 --> 00:09:03,159
and she looks at you a little longer than he does.

244
00:09:03,159 --> 00:09:04,520
She gives you a piece of

245
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:06,181
bread for the walk back.

246
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:07,456
It is kind.

247
00:09:07,456 --> 00:09:09,778
It does not change anything.

248
00:09:10,297 --> 00:09:11,494
This is the uncomfortable

249
00:09:11,494 --> 00:09:12,990
part of the Viking story.

250
00:09:13,525 --> 00:09:14,887
Eirik is no monster.

251
00:09:14,887 --> 00:09:16,862
He is an ordinary farmer who

252
00:09:16,862 --> 00:09:19,306
went on one raid, came home with a

253
00:09:19,306 --> 00:09:21,795
cut arm and a small pouch of silver,

254
00:09:21,795 --> 00:09:23,544
and went back to his fences.

255
00:09:23,544 --> 00:09:25,008
And some of that silver

256
00:09:25,008 --> 00:09:26,990
exists because you were sold.

257
00:09:26,990 --> 00:09:28,809
He was far from alone.

258
00:09:29,248 --> 00:09:32,235
Hundreds of thousands of Islamic silver coins,

259
00:09:32,235 --> 00:09:33,179
called dirhams,

260
00:09:33,179 --> 00:09:36,256
have been found in hoards across northern Europe.

261
00:09:36,256 --> 00:09:38,756
The Swedish History Museum suggests that

262
00:09:38,756 --> 00:09:41,722
many of them may partly reflect the slave trade,

263
00:09:41,722 --> 00:09:43,699
and a research project at the

264
00:09:43,699 --> 00:09:45,390
University of Oxford explored the

265
00:09:45,390 --> 00:09:47,942
possibility that captives, rather than furs,

266
00:09:47,942 --> 00:09:50,687
were the main goods exchanged for that silver.

267
00:09:51,092 --> 00:09:52,384
Historians still debate

268
00:09:52,384 --> 00:09:54,084
how large that trade was.

269
00:09:54,537 --> 00:09:55,649
But the silver never cared

270
00:09:55,649 --> 00:09:57,144
who had been weighed for it.

271
00:09:57,485 --> 00:09:59,045
Walking back down the valley,

272
00:09:59,045 --> 00:10:00,333
you eat the bread.

273
00:10:00,771 --> 00:10:02,852
Then you go back to the dung baskets.

274
00:10:02,852 --> 00:10:04,376
In early spring,

275
00:10:04,376 --> 00:10:06,215
Hallvard's old father dies.

276
00:10:06,215 --> 00:10:08,322
The household builds him a grave

277
00:10:08,322 --> 00:10:10,526
mound and lays him in it with his sword,

278
00:10:10,526 --> 00:10:13,362
his drinking horn and his best cloak.

279
00:10:13,362 --> 00:10:15,405
Ketil goes very quiet.

280
00:10:15,844 --> 00:10:18,144
That night, he tells you why.

281
00:10:18,144 --> 00:10:19,633
Some Viking graves

282
00:10:19,633 --> 00:10:21,420
hold more than one person.

283
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:23,971
The National Museum of Denmark

284
00:10:23,971 --> 00:10:25,714
mentions burials in which slaves were

285
00:10:25,714 --> 00:10:27,849
forced to follow their owners in death.

286
00:10:28,384 --> 00:10:30,513
The Swedish History Museum says that

287
00:10:30,513 --> 00:10:33,273
some double graves may contain a thrall

288
00:10:33,273 --> 00:10:35,422
who went into the grave with an owner.

289
00:10:35,422 --> 00:10:37,706
At Flakstad, in the Lofoten islands

290
00:10:37,706 --> 00:10:40,369
of northern Norway, a team led by the

291
00:10:40,369 --> 00:10:42,563
archaeologist Elise Naumann of the

292
00:10:42,563 --> 00:10:46,561
University of Oslo re-examined ten skeletons from single,

293
00:10:46,561 --> 00:10:48,542
double and triple graves.

294
00:10:48,915 --> 00:10:50,089
At least three of the people

295
00:10:50,089 --> 00:10:51,262
in the shared graves had

296
00:10:51,262 --> 00:10:53,414
been buried without their heads.

297
00:10:53,414 --> 00:10:54,896
DNA showed that the people

298
00:10:54,896 --> 00:10:57,147
buried together were most likely

299
00:10:57,147 --> 00:10:58,896
not related through their mothers.

300
00:10:58,896 --> 00:11:01,196
And chemical traces in the bones showed

301
00:11:01,196 --> 00:11:03,582
that the headless people had eaten mostly fish,

302
00:11:03,582 --> 00:11:05,049
while others in the graves had

303
00:11:05,049 --> 00:11:07,479
eaten much more meat and food from the land.

304
00:11:07,982 --> 00:11:09,426
The researchers suggest that the

305
00:11:09,426 --> 00:11:11,289
headless people were slaves,

306
00:11:11,289 --> 00:11:13,793
killed and buried with their masters.

307
00:11:13,793 --> 00:11:16,375
It is a hypothesis, not a certainty.

308
00:11:16,375 --> 00:11:18,272
The graves were only partly

309
00:11:18,272 --> 00:11:20,361
excavated in the nineteen eighties,

310
00:11:20,361 --> 00:11:22,164
and may have been disturbed.

311
00:11:22,164 --> 00:11:23,594
And such killings seem

312
00:11:23,594 --> 00:11:25,076
to have been uncommon.

313
00:11:25,076 --> 00:11:26,654
But the fear in Ketil's

314
00:11:26,654 --> 00:11:28,952
voice is not a hypothesis.

315
00:11:28,952 --> 00:11:31,573
Hallvard's father goes into his mound alone,

316
00:11:31,573 --> 00:11:33,380
with his sword and his cloak.

317
00:11:33,380 --> 00:11:35,418
You stand at the back with Ketil,

318
00:11:35,418 --> 00:11:36,959
and you breathe again.

319
00:11:36,959 --> 00:11:39,064
Then it is time to plough.

320
00:11:39,064 --> 00:11:40,937
Hallvard's ox is lame,

321
00:11:40,937 --> 00:11:42,631
the field cannot wait,

322
00:11:42,988 --> 00:11:45,749
and so you and Ketil are put in the harness.

323
00:11:45,749 --> 00:11:47,896
You drag the plough across the stony

324
00:11:47,896 --> 00:11:50,769
field while Hallvard's son walks behind it,

325
00:11:50,769 --> 00:11:53,643
giving directions the way he would to an ox.

326
00:11:53,643 --> 00:11:56,172
That may sound like an exaggeration.

327
00:11:56,172 --> 00:11:58,213
But Iceland's Book of Settlements,

328
00:11:58,213 --> 00:12:00,097
written down centuries later,

329
00:12:00,097 --> 00:12:03,549
tells of a settler named Hjörleif who had only one ox,

330
00:12:03,549 --> 00:12:06,075
and made his thralls drag the plough.

331
00:12:06,559 --> 00:12:07,792
Remember Hjörleif.

332
00:12:07,792 --> 00:12:10,187
He comes back in this story.

333
00:12:10,187 --> 00:12:11,466
Ketil has a plan,

334
00:12:11,466 --> 00:12:13,217
and it involves counting.

335
00:12:13,217 --> 00:12:14,894
He is a debt thrall,

336
00:12:14,894 --> 00:12:16,905
and debt thralldom could be temporary.

337
00:12:16,905 --> 00:12:19,047
If he pays back what he owes,

338
00:12:19,047 --> 00:12:21,474
he believes, Hallvard must let him go.

339
00:12:21,474 --> 00:12:23,092
So every evening,

340
00:12:23,092 --> 00:12:27,186
after the day's work, Ketil carves wooden spoons and bowls,

341
00:12:27,186 --> 00:12:29,296
and trades them for scraps of silver.

342
00:12:29,296 --> 00:12:31,395
He keeps them in a little cloth bag,

343
00:12:31,395 --> 00:12:32,691
hidden in the roof.

344
00:12:32,691 --> 00:12:34,376
He counts them more often

345
00:12:34,376 --> 00:12:36,388
than Hallvard counts his cows.

346
00:12:36,388 --> 00:12:39,442
Could thralls really buy their way out?

347
00:12:39,442 --> 00:12:39,818
Yes.

348
00:12:39,818 --> 00:12:42,651
The National Museum of Denmark

349
00:12:42,651 --> 00:12:44,395
notes that slaves could have their

350
00:12:44,395 --> 00:12:45,641
freedom bought by others,

351
00:12:45,641 --> 00:12:48,970
or in some cases be set free by their owners.

352
00:12:48,970 --> 00:12:51,134
Historians also describe

353
00:12:51,134 --> 00:12:53,123
thralls who bought their own freedom.

354
00:12:53,123 --> 00:12:54,500
A saga written in the twelve

355
00:12:54,500 --> 00:12:57,255
hundreds describes the most generous version.

356
00:12:57,629 --> 00:12:59,162
It says that the Norwegian

357
00:12:59,162 --> 00:13:01,226
chieftain Erling Skjalgsson,

358
00:13:01,226 --> 00:13:04,328
who lived a couple of generations after your time,

359
00:13:04,328 --> 00:13:06,522
kept thirty slaves on his farm.

360
00:13:06,522 --> 00:13:08,949
He gave each a set day's work,

361
00:13:08,949 --> 00:13:10,578
then let them work for themselves

362
00:13:10,578 --> 00:13:12,562
in the twilight and at night,

363
00:13:12,562 --> 00:13:14,755
with land to grow their own grain.

364
00:13:14,755 --> 00:13:17,009
Each was given a set amount

365
00:13:17,009 --> 00:13:18,785
of work to earn their freedom.

366
00:13:18,785 --> 00:13:20,569
According to the saga,

367
00:13:20,569 --> 00:13:23,500
many managed it within one or two years,

368
00:13:23,874 --> 00:13:26,565
and anyone with any luck within three.

369
00:13:27,084 --> 00:13:28,337
With that money,

370
00:13:28,337 --> 00:13:30,326
Erling bought more slaves.

371
00:13:30,326 --> 00:13:32,355
Read that last sentence again.

372
00:13:32,355 --> 00:13:34,274
Even the kindest example in the

373
00:13:34,274 --> 00:13:36,393
sources is still a business plan.

374
00:13:36,393 --> 00:13:38,155
And historians are not sure the

375
00:13:38,155 --> 00:13:40,729
saga's picture is reliable at all.

376
00:13:40,729 --> 00:13:43,414
Store norske leksikon calls it uncertain.

377
00:13:43,414 --> 00:13:45,609
Some owners gave freedom away.

378
00:13:45,609 --> 00:13:47,743
At Hørning, in Denmark,

379
00:13:47,743 --> 00:13:51,645
a farmer ploughing his field in eighteen forty-nine found a

380
00:13:51,645 --> 00:13:54,690
runestone raised by a man called Tóki the smith,

381
00:13:54,690 --> 00:13:56,998
in memory of a man named Thorgisl.

382
00:13:56,998 --> 00:13:58,717
The inscription says that

383
00:13:58,717 --> 00:14:00,143
Thorgisl gave him gold,

384
00:14:00,143 --> 00:14:02,953
though that word is uncertain, and freedom.

385
00:14:03,296 --> 00:14:05,081
A man who had once been unfree

386
00:14:05,081 --> 00:14:07,472
paid for a stone in his own name.

387
00:14:07,472 --> 00:14:09,162
That is a real success story,

388
00:14:09,162 --> 00:14:11,095
carved for everyone to read.

389
00:14:11,405 --> 00:14:13,256
But freedom came with strings.

390
00:14:13,256 --> 00:14:15,723
A freed thrall was called a leysingi,

391
00:14:16,060 --> 00:14:18,768
and still owed loyalty to the former master.

392
00:14:19,206 --> 00:14:21,394
Store norske leksikon notes that

393
00:14:21,394 --> 00:14:23,694
the laws expected it to take

394
00:14:23,694 --> 00:14:25,502
several generations before a freed

395
00:14:25,502 --> 00:14:27,943
family was fully free of those ties.

396
00:14:27,943 --> 00:14:30,888
So even Ketil's plan has a long tail.

397
00:14:30,888 --> 00:14:33,214
His grandchildren may still owe

398
00:14:33,214 --> 00:14:35,508
Hallvard's grandchildren a favour.

399
00:14:35,508 --> 00:14:37,305
Which brings us back to Hjörleif.

400
00:14:37,808 --> 00:14:39,734
The Book of Settlements says he raided

401
00:14:39,734 --> 00:14:41,910
in Ireland and took ten thralls there.

402
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:43,747
Then he sailed with his

403
00:14:43,747 --> 00:14:46,552
foster-brother Ingólf to settle Iceland,

404
00:14:46,552 --> 00:14:47,833
in the eight hundred and seventies.

405
00:14:47,833 --> 00:14:49,600
In the spring,

406
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:51,530
with one ox and a field to sow,

407
00:14:51,530 --> 00:14:54,271
he made his Irish thralls drag the plough.

408
00:14:54,271 --> 00:14:55,204
One of them,

409
00:14:55,204 --> 00:14:58,048
a man named Dufthak, came up with a plan.

410
00:14:58,048 --> 00:14:59,369
They killed the ox and

411
00:14:59,369 --> 00:15:01,154
said that a bear had done it.

412
00:15:01,154 --> 00:15:03,097
When Hjörleif and his men spread

413
00:15:03,097 --> 00:15:05,249
out into the woods to hunt the bear,

414
00:15:05,249 --> 00:15:07,587
the thralls attacked and killed them.

415
00:15:07,977 --> 00:15:09,979
Then they fled by boat to islands they

416
00:15:09,979 --> 00:15:12,794
could see out at sea, to the southwest.

417
00:15:12,794 --> 00:15:15,285
Ingólf found Hjörleif's body,

418
00:15:15,285 --> 00:15:17,851
guessed where the boat had gone, and followed.

419
00:15:17,851 --> 00:15:20,236
He found the thralls on the islands,

420
00:15:20,236 --> 00:15:21,309
and killed them all.

421
00:15:21,309 --> 00:15:23,344
The Norse called the Irish Westmen.

422
00:15:23,344 --> 00:15:26,074
So the islands were named Vestmannaeyjar,

423
00:15:26,528 --> 00:15:27,544
the Westman Islands,

424
00:15:27,544 --> 00:15:29,586
and that is still their name today.

425
00:15:30,039 --> 00:15:32,079
Remember who wrote this story down:

426
00:15:32,079 --> 00:15:34,462
the descendants of the settlers,

427
00:15:34,462 --> 00:15:38,095
centuries later, not the descendants of the thralls.

428
00:15:38,548 --> 00:15:40,899
We will never hear Dufthak's version.

429
00:15:41,433 --> 00:15:43,976
Thralldom did not vanish overnight.

430
00:15:43,976 --> 00:15:46,374
The National Museum of Denmark

431
00:15:46,374 --> 00:15:49,232
notes that slavery declined with Christianity,

432
00:15:49,589 --> 00:15:50,545
but was not fully

433
00:15:50,545 --> 00:15:52,890
abolished until the Middle Ages.

434
00:15:52,890 --> 00:15:53,704
In Sweden,

435
00:15:53,704 --> 00:15:58,344
thralldom was formally abolished in its last remaining

436
00:15:58,344 --> 00:16:00,120
provinces in thirteen thirty-five.

437
00:16:00,676 --> 00:16:01,909
That is almost four hundred

438
00:16:01,909 --> 00:16:04,291
years after you were weighed

439
00:16:04,291 --> 00:16:06,211
on a scale at a trading place.

440
00:16:06,211 --> 00:16:07,821
So why was being a Viking

441
00:16:07,821 --> 00:16:09,878
thrall worse than you think?

442
00:16:09,878 --> 00:16:12,798
Because you were in the story all along,

443
00:16:12,798 --> 00:16:14,361
and nobody wrote you in.

444
00:16:14,361 --> 00:16:15,994
You built the fences,

445
00:16:15,994 --> 00:16:19,205
carried the dung, cut the turf and pulled the plough,

446
00:16:19,622 --> 00:16:20,888
and the poems named

447
00:16:20,888 --> 00:16:23,214
your children Horsefly and Log.

448
00:16:23,637 --> 00:16:25,930
And yet, some thralls did get out.

449
00:16:25,930 --> 00:16:27,999
Some bought their freedom,

450
00:16:28,309 --> 00:16:31,256
some were given it, and one freed smith

451
00:16:31,256 --> 00:16:33,313
in Denmark raised a stone that

452
00:16:33,313 --> 00:16:35,770
you can still see in a museum today.

453
00:16:36,225 --> 00:16:37,430
Most, like you,

454
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:40,539
simply kept going, which may be the

455
00:16:40,539 --> 00:16:42,666
hardest thing in this whole story.

456
00:16:42,666 --> 00:16:43,335
By summer,

457
00:16:43,335 --> 00:16:46,598
Ketil's bag in the roof is a little heavier.

458
00:16:46,598 --> 00:16:47,136
Hallvard,

459
00:16:47,136 --> 00:16:50,502
to everyone's surprise, has given you a strip of

460
00:16:50,502 --> 00:16:52,994
stony ground to dig in the evenings.

461
00:16:52,994 --> 00:16:54,638
Maybe in three years.

462
00:16:55,011 --> 00:16:56,152
Maybe never.

463
00:16:56,152 --> 00:16:57,293
Next spring,

464
00:16:57,293 --> 00:17:00,238
Bjorn will talk about another voyage.

465
00:17:00,238 --> 00:17:02,381
You will hear about it from the field,

466
00:17:02,706 --> 00:17:04,002
and you will know exactly

467
00:17:04,002 --> 00:17:06,196
what it means for somebody's village.

468
00:17:06,617 --> 00:17:08,477
And somewhere up the valley,

469
00:17:08,477 --> 00:17:10,829
Eirik's sheep has escaped again.

470
00:17:11,205 --> 00:17:13,522
Guess who gets sent to bring it back.

471
00:17:13,522 --> 00:17:15,645
This is Viking Chapters:

472
00:17:15,645 --> 00:17:17,397
the lives behind the legends.

473
00:17:17,397 --> 00:17:19,324
If you would like to see another

474
00:17:19,324 --> 00:17:20,843
life behind the legends,

475
00:17:20,843 --> 00:17:24,133
subscribe, and we will meet in the next chapter.
